> Are we 100% sure that this is the correct place to do this?

I'm not so sure anymore. I don't have a good idea how long one run of `syncLoopIteration()` takes, but there might be some risk that it prevents a notify in time for low watchdog timeouts?

@sftim @odinuge What do you think about something like this instead, in `syncLoop()`? This will run the watchdog notify in its own loop, and it stops notifying in case the `syncLoopMonitor` wasn't updated for some time.

```
	// If the starting systemd unit asks for a watchdog, notify periodically
	if watchdogDuration, err := daemon.SdWatchdogEnabled(false); watchdogDuration != 0 || err != nil {
		switch {
		case err != nil:
			klog.Errorf("Reading watchdog configuration failed: %v", err)
			break
		case watchdogDuration != 0:
			go wait.Forever(func(){
				// check syncLoopMonitor: if it reports an old time, something is going wrong
				healthyPeriod := 30 * time.Second
				if kl.LatestLoopEntryTime().Before(time.Now().Add(-healthyPeriod)) {
					klog.Errorf("syncLoopMonitor not updated for at least %v, not going to notify watchdog", healthyPeriod)
				} else {
					_, _ = daemon.SdNotify(false, daemon.SdNotifyWatchdog)
				}
			}, watchdogDuration / 3)
			break
		}
	}

